<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  OnScreen is designed to talk to a SELF-HOSTED media server, which the
  operator typically runs on a LAN address — `http://192.168.1.50:7070`,
  `http://media.lan:7070`, `http://onscreen-server.local:7070`. Plain HTTP
  on the LAN is the expected deployment shape; Android refuses to dial
  cleartext URLs unless explicitly allowed here. Internet-side hops
  (TMDB, OpenSubtitles, etc.) still go over HTTPS — those URLs come from
  the server, which itself uses a `safehttp`-policy-restricted client.

  We accept the `InsecureBaseConfiguration` lint warning consciously:
  flipping cleartext off would break the dominant deployment model for
  a marginal threat (an attacker already on the user's LAN). Server-
  side TLS support is in Settings ▸ Security; operators who serve over
  HTTPS get certificate validation on the client automatically.
-->
<network-security-config xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools">
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"
        tools:ignore="InsecureBaseConfiguration">
        <!--
          SYSTEM CAs ONLY. `<certificates src="user" />` used to be here; it is
          deliberately gone.

          Android stopped trusting the user CA store by default in API 24
          precisely because it is the easiest MITM foothold on the platform: a
          sideloaded "helper" app that walks the owner through installing a
          profile, an MDM enrolment, or a few minutes of ADB access is enough to
          plant one. Sideloading is routine on Fire TV. With that anchor in
          place every HTTPS call to the server was interceptable, which means
          the login password and the PASETO access / stream / asset tokens were
          all readable by whoever planted the CA.

          It also contradicted the paragraph above: operators serving over TLS
          were told they "get certificate validation on the client
          automatically", and that was not true while any user-installed CA
          could sign for the server's hostname.

          The cleartext allowance above is a considered trade for the LAN
          deployment model. This was not — it had no rationale, and it silently
          voided the protection the TLS path exists to provide.
        -->
        <!--
          KNOWN GAP — API 23. android:networkSecurityConfig is honored from
          API 24 only, and this module's minSdk is 23 (app/build.gradle.kts).
          On an Android 6 device this whole file is ignored, and the platform
          default there still trusts the user CA store — so the removal above
          buys nothing on that slice of Fire TV hardware. Closing it means
          minSdk 24, which drops those devices; that is a reach decision, not
          a code one. Scope of the residual risk is narrow: it needs an
          Android 6 TV, a server reached over HTTPS (LAN installs are
          cleartext by design and unaffected), and a planted CA.
        -->
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>
</network-security-config>
